          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you (here: I love you as a sister loves a brother).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly avows a loving state, explicitly qualified as sisterly rather than romantic love. Jane presents this affection as one reason she would have trusted St. John as a companion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The love expression itself does not undertake marriage, travel, missionary service, or any continuing obligation. Jane instead denies obligation and distinguishes her sisterly affection from the separate, conditional proposal that she accompany St. John.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"This is a deliberate explanation of Jane's motives and the kind of affection she bears St. John, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Its core force is adequately represented as a qualified avowal of sisterly affection. The phrase helps contrast that affection with marital love, but this relational qualification does not require a separate residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is arguing with St. John after refusing to become his wife and disputing his claim that she has promised to go to India. She explains that she would have risked much specifically with him because she admires and trusts him and loves him in a sisterly capacity, while denying romantic love, marital consent, and any binding obligation to travel.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression undertakes nothing. Nearby, Jane had offered to accompany St. John as an assistant or sister, but in this exchange she expressly denies any formal promise or obligation and says she may remain in England. St. John rejects the unmarried arrangement and treats her refusal of marriage as ending their proposed joint mission; there is no clear uptake of her sisterly avowal itself.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre. This classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence clearly establishes the represented sisterly avowal and rejection of marriage. It does not independently establish St. John's private interpretation of the avowal beyond his stated belief that Jane's heart turns toward Mr. Rochester.\"}"
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "role": "assistant"
    }
